const articleHtml = "<h2>What the Limit of Detection Actually Describes</h2>\n<p>A limit of detection is a statement about probability, not a promise. When a laboratory reports detection at 10 copies per reaction, the full claim is usually: 10 copies were found in at least 95% of replicate reactions, in the matrix tested, using the stated extraction and amplification protocol. Remove those conditions and the number stops meaning much.</p>\n<p>Avian matrices make the conditions unusually important. Feather pulp, dried blood cards, cloacal swabs, and tissue carry different DNA quantities, different inhibitors, and different amounts of non-target avian genome. An assay that reaches five copies in a clean plasmid dilution may need several hundred copies in a melanin-rich feather extract.</p>\n<p>The figure describes an analytical event, not a biological one. It answers \"can this method see the molecule?\" not \"is this bird infected?\" or \"is this bird female?\" Those questions have separate measures, covered in the wider guide to <a href=\"/bird-dna-testing-methods-accuracy/\">bird DNA testing methods and accuracy</a>.</p>\n<h2>LoB, LoD, and LoQ: Three Different Questions</h2>\n<p>The three terms form a hierarchy, and laboratories sometimes use them loosely. They are not synonyms.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>Typical statistical basis</th>\n<th>Avian example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit of blank (LoB)</td>\n<td>What signal do target-free samples produce?</td>\n<td>Mean plus 1.645 SD of blanks, or the 95th percentile of observed blanks</td>\n<td>A no-template control showing faint late fluorescence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit of detection (LoD)</td>\n<td>What is the lowest level detected with a stated probability?</td>\n<td>LoB plus 1.645 SD of low-level replicates, or probit regression</td>\n<td>10 copies of PBFD DNA per reaction, detected in 95% of replicates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit of quantification (LoQ)</td>\n<td>What is the lowest level measured with acceptable precision?</td>\n<td>Lowest level meeting a predefined CV, often 20–25%</td>\n<td>Separating 200 from 2,000 copies in a viral load series</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>LoB is the noise floor. LoD sits above it and describes detection. LoQ sits above LoD and describes measurement. A method can detect without quantifying: at ten copies, three of three replicates may amplify while their Cq values scatter across two cycles. That is a detection result. Reporting it as a viral load would overstate the data.</p>\n<p>LoB also depends on blank replicate count. Three wells cannot estimate a 95th percentile, which is why a serious summary states the replicate number next to the estimate.</p>\n<h2>How a Laboratory Establishes an LoD for an Avian Assay</h2>\n<p>A defensible study starts with the matrix, not the target. Spiking a plasmid into buffer measures chemistry. Spiking it into pooled negative feather extract measures something closer to real submissions.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Define matrix and input.</strong> State how much feather material, blood card punch, or swab eluate enters extraction, and how much extract enters the reaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantify the target independently.</strong> Plasmid stocks, in vitro transcripts, or synthetic double-stranded DNA of known copy number. An unquantified positive diluted tenfold gives relative levels, not copies.</li>\n<li><strong>Bracket the transition.</strong> If detection fails between 1 and 100 copies, the informative levels sit inside that range, not at 10,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Run enough replicates.</strong> Twenty per level is common in validation work. Five produce wide confidence intervals.</li>\n<li><strong>Fit the model.</strong> Probit regression gives the 95% detection level directly; the SD-based formula assumes constant variance at low levels.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirm independently.</strong> A second series on another day catches pipetting bias and preparation errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For RNA viruses, reverse transcription belongs inside the measurement. RT efficiency varies with priming strategy, transcript structure, and input volume, so an LoD quoted for \"the qPCR\" without RT conditions is incomplete. That difference is part of the <a href=\"/rt-pcr-vs-traditional-pcr-in-a-bird-dna-testing-lab/\">RT-PCR versus traditional PCR</a> comparison. For DNA targets such as PBFD virus or CHD, extraction recovery carries more of the variability.</p>\n<p>Endpoint PCR with gel detection follows a different convention: an input mass, such as 1–10 ng of DNA, and a visible band threshold rather than a copy number. That is legitimate for a classification assay but not comparable to a qPCR LoD in copies per reaction.</p>\n<h2>Analytical Sensitivity and Diagnostic Sensitivity Are Not Interchangeable</h2>\n<p>Analytical sensitivity is the detection capability above. Diagnostic sensitivity is the share of genuinely positive animals that the whole pathway calls positive. The two are related, and mixing them is the commonest source of confusion in marketing.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>Analytical sensitivity</th>\n<th>Diagnostic sensitivity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What it measures</td>\n<td>Smallest amount of target the method can detect</td>\n<td>Share of true positives the pathway reports as positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Usual unit</td>\n<td>Copies per reaction or µL, or ng of input DNA</td>\n<td>Percent, with a confidence interval</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depends on</td>\n<td>Primers, probes, chemistry, instrument, matrix</td>\n<td>Sampling site, timing, transport, extraction, assay, calling rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Improved by</td>\n<td>Better chemistry, more input, more replicates</td>\n<td>Better sampling, validated cutoffs, species-matched primers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fails when</td>\n<td>Target sits below the LoD</td>\n<td>Target never entered the sample, inhibitor present, primer mismatch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An assay with a five-copy LoD can still miss an infected bird if the swab never touched infected tissue, or if the bird was sampled before shedding began. Diagnostic sensitivity belongs to the whole pathway. The LoD is one term in that chain, and often not the limiting one. Our overview of <a href=\"/avian-virus-rt-pcr-test/\">avian virus qPCR testing</a> shows how the steps connect.</p>\n<h2>Where LoD Matters: Pathogen Screening in Feathers and Swabs</h2>\n<p>Viral shedding fluctuates. A PBFD carrier may yield a strong signal from one feather follicle and nothing from another on the same bird. A pigeon infected with PMV-1 may shed intermittently. In that setting, the LoD decides what \"not detected\" means: below the assay's floor, or absent from the material submitted.</p>\n<p>That is why careful reports say \"not detected\" rather than \"negative.\" The first is a statement about assay and sample. The second is a statement about the bird, and it needs assumptions the laboratory cannot verify. The same logic runs through <a href=\"/how-feather-pcr-testing-works-for-pbfd-and-avian-polyomavirus/\">PBFD and polyomavirus feather testing</a>, where sample quality varies more than chemistry.</p>\n<p>Pooling shifts the effective limit. Combine five swabs into one extraction and a low-level positive is diluted fivefold before the reaction starts. The assay's LoD is unchanged; the LoD per bird is not. Any pooled screening program should calculate that shift instead of assuming the individual figure carries over.</p>\n<h2>Where LoD Matters Less: CHD Sexing Is a Classification Task</h2>\n<p>CHD sexing asks a different question. Both sexes carry CHD-Z; females also carry CHD-W. The assay does not need to find one molecule in a crowded background. It needs a pattern that can be assigned to one of two classes without ambiguity.</p>\n<p>The failure modes follow from that. Primer mismatch in a species whose CHD sequence diverges at the binding site. CHD-W and CHD-Z fragments too close to separate on the chosen platform. Heteroduplex formation between CHD-Z variants. Allele dropout at very low input. Female DNA contamination. Interpretation error. A lower LoD addresses dropout and nothing else on that list.</p>\n<p>Very low input raises the risk that the W fragment fails while Z succeeds, producing a single-band pattern in a female. Better detection can reduce that specific failure. The honest claim is narrow: improved sensitivity lowers dropout risk at low input. The claim that does not follow is that the lowest-LoD assay is the better sexing test overall. Fragment resolution, species coverage, and calling rules decide more cases. The <a href=\"/the-complete-guide-to-bird-dna-sexing/\">complete guide to bird DNA sexing</a> covers the marker biology, and the <a href=\"/avian-gender-dna-test/\">gender testing service page</a> describes the customer-facing workflow.</p>\n<h2>Why a Lower LoD Does Not Automatically Mean a Better Sexing Test</h2>\n<p>A laboratory can hold every statement below true at once, and none of them establishes a correct sex call across species.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>What it supports</th>\n<th>What it does not support</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"LoD of 5 copies per reaction\"</td>\n<td>Detection of trace target in the tested matrix</td>\n<td>Correct classification of CHD patterns in a new species</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"qPCR is more sensitive than gel PCR\"</td>\n<td>Earlier signal, less post-amplification handling</td>\n<td>Resolution of two fragments a few base pairs apart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Works from 0.1 ng of DNA\"</td>\n<td>Tolerance of low input</td>\n<td>Freedom from melanin or humic inhibition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"99% concordance\"</td>\n<td>Agreement inside the validation set</td>\n<td>Agreement in a species or sample type outside it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The measurements that rank sexing assays are less photogenic: species validated, reference samples per species, concordance with independently established sex, inconclusive rate, retest rate, and the resolution method used for close fragments. Capillary electrophoresis is often the deciding upgrade when two products differ by only a few base pairs, as the <a href=\"/capillary-electrophoresis-vs-traditional-gel-electrophoresis-which-should-your-bird-dna-lab-use/\">gel versus capillary comparison</a> explains.</p>\n<h2>Sample Quality, Inhibition, and the Effective Detection Limit</h2>\n<p>Every real sample has an effective limit worse than the catalog value. Feather melanin binds polymerase and quenches fluorescence. Humic compounds from environmental swabs do the same. EDTA chelates magnesium, heparin inhibits polymerase directly, and heat or humidity during shipping fragments template.</p>\n<p>An internal amplification control is the practical check. When it amplifies late in a sample that also shows a late target signal, the sample is the likely problem. When it fails outright, the run says nothing about the target, and \"not detected\" would be unsupported. Extraction blanks catch contamination introduced before amplification, which a no-template control cannot see.</p>\n<p>Sensitivity cuts both ways. An assay tuned to find one molecule will find the one that drifted from the adjacent bench. Carryover, contaminated reagents, and shared pipettes matter more as the LoD falls. The useful question is not how low a laboratory can push the number, but how low it can go while negative controls behave. That balance belongs to the quality system in our <a href=\"/scientific-regulatory-compliance-statement/\">scientific and regulatory compliance statement</a>, and sample handling is covered in the <a href=\"/sample-shipping/\">shipping guidance</a>.</p>\n<h2>Reading an LoD Claim: Units, Matrix, and Protocol</h2>\n<p>Most comparisons between laboratories fail before the arithmetic starts, because the units differ. Copies per reaction, copies per microliter of extract, and copies per milliliter of blood are different quantities connected by input and elution volumes.</p>\n<p>A worked example makes the point. Laboratory A reports 10 copies per reaction using 5 µL of extract in a 20 µL reaction: 2 copies per microliter. Laboratory B reports 40 copies per reaction using 10 µL in a 25 µL reaction: 4 copies per microliter. Per microliter of extract, B is more sensitive despite the larger headline number.</p>\n<p>Seven questions sort out most claims:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Copies per what: reaction, microliter of eluate, or milliliter of sample?</li>\n<li>How much extract entered the reaction, and how much sample entered extraction?</li>\n<li>Spiked into buffer, pooled negative matrix, or individual field samples?</li>\n<li>How many replicates per level, and how many blanks?</li>\n<li>Is the figure the 95% detection level or a single observed hit?</li>\n<li>Was it confirmed on an independent dilution series?</li>\n<li>For RNA targets, were RT efficiency and priming included?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A laboratory that answers these without hesitation has done the work. One that answers with a percentage and a slogan has not shown anything yet.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Is limit of detection the same as sensitivity?</h3>\n<p>Only in the analytical sense. Analytical sensitivity is often defined as the LoD. Diagnostic sensitivity is the proportion of true positives detected by the full pathway. A method can have excellent analytical sensitivity and mediocre diagnostic sensitivity if sampling is poor.</p>\n<h3>Can an assay detect a target below its stated LoD?</h3>\n<p>Sometimes, by chance. Below the 95% detection level, amplification becomes stochastic: some replicates amplify, most do not. An occasional hit is expected behavior, not evidence the LoD is wrong, and not a reliable basis for a reported result.</p>\n<h3>Why do two laboratories report different LoDs for the same virus?</h3>\n<p>Different primers and probes, extraction chemistry, input volumes, matrices, replicate counts, and definitions of the detection level. Without the protocol, the two numbers cannot be ranked.</p>\n<h3>Does a lower LoD mean fewer false negatives?</h3>\n<p>It reduces false negatives caused by concentrations near the old detection floor. It does not fix those caused by poor sampling, inhibition, or primer mismatch. In field testing, sampling usually dominates.</p>\n<h3>Is LoD relevant to bird sexing at all?</h3>\n<p>To one failure mode: allele dropout at very low input. It says little about fragment resolution, species-specific primer performance, heteroduplex artifacts, or interpretation. Sexing is a classification problem, judged by concordance and inconclusive rate.</p>\n<h3>What is the difference between LoD and LoQ?</h3>\n<p>LoD is the lowest level detected with a stated probability. LoQ is the lowest level measured with acceptable precision and bias. A laboratory may detect 10 copies and quantify only above 200. Viral load reporting should stay above the LoQ.</p>\n<h3>What should I ask a laboratory about its detection limit?</h3>\n<p>Ask for the matrix, input volume, replicate count, statistical definition, and independent confirmation. Then ask what the figure does not cover, such as species outside the validation panel. The second answer is usually more informative.</p>\n<h2>The Author's Take</h2>\n<p><strong>Position.</strong> A limit of detection is a narrow, conditional statement and should never stand in for test quality. In avian work, the useful ranking question is whether the assay was validated for the species and sample type in front of you.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> I have seen LoD figures in copies per reaction placed beside competitor figures in copies per milliliter as if the two were comparable. I have also seen sexing services advertise detection sensitivity as though it predicted classification accuracy, when the dominant CHD error modes are primer mismatch, poor fragment resolution, and heteroduplex artifacts. The number is real; the inference usually is not. A laboratory that publishes matrix, input, replicate count, and inconclusive rate gives a customer something actionable. One that publishes a single figure gives them a mood.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure.</strong> This is editorial judgment about reading technical claims, not a certification of any laboratory or guarantee of any result. It is written for avian research, breeding, and veterinary discussion, not human medical guidance.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Armbruster, D. A., &amp; Pry, T. (2008). Limit of blank, limit of detection and limit of quantitation. <em>Clinical Biochemist Reviews</em>, 29(Suppl 1), S49–S52. <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2556583/\">PMC2556583</a></li>\n<li>Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. <em>EP17: Evaluation of Detection Capability for Clinical Laboratory Measurement Procedures</em>. <a href=\"https://clsi.org/standards/products/method-evaluation/documents/ep17/\">CLSI</a></li>\n<li>Bustin, S. A., et al. (2009). The MIQE guidelines. <em>Clinical Chemistry</em>, 55(4), 611–622. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1373/clinchem.2008.112797\">doi:10.1373/clinchem.2008.112797</a></li>\n<li>Forootan, A., et al. (2017). Methods to determine limit of detection and limit of quantification in quantitative real-time PCR. <em>Biomolecular Detection and Quantification</em>, 12, 1–6. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bdq.2017.04.001\">doi:10.1016/j.bdq.2017.04.001</a></li>\n<li>Klymus, K. E., et al. (2020). Reporting the limits of detection and quantification for environmental DNA assays. <em>Environmental DNA</em>, 2(3), 271–282. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/edn3.29\">doi:10.1002/edn3.29</a></li>\n<li>Griffiths, R., Double, M. C., Orr, K., &amp; Dawson, R. J. G. (1998). A DNA test to sex most birds. <em>Molecular Ecology</em>, 7(8), 1071–1075. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-294x.1998.00389.x\">doi:10.1046/j.1365-294x.1998.00389.x</a></li>\n<li>Morinha, F., Cabral, J. A., &amp; Bastos, E. (2012). Molecular sexing of birds: a comparative review of PCR-based methods. <em>Theriogenology</em>, 78(4), 703–714. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.theriogenology.2012.04.015\">doi:10.1016/j.theriogenology.2012.04.015</a></li>\n</ol>";
